{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED exact head 5bea80cbe72f41a2bff49336d103269db7e52c05. The isolated package, nine-key runtime identity, module-layout decision, and existing tests are well structured, and go test ./..., go vet ./..., go test -race ./..., ./scripts/demo.sh, and diff checking all pass in a clean exact-head worktree. Three contract gaps prevent approval. First, the adopted note makes authority part of Dialect and specifically requires Tailapp's read-visibility rules; this type has no authority/read-visibility policy, leaving the owner checks still hard-coded in internal/profile/compiler.go and forcing Stage 4 either to hard-code Tailapp in core or break this contract. Topology also does not encode the required exactly-one normalizer and at-least-one fold cardinalities. Second, EventContract is not a typed scalar envelope: []string records names only and cannot express each field's logical type/nullability. Third, the consistency tests do not use HostEvent.Name or HostEvent.ScalarFields at all (dialectSources uses Topology.NormalizerConsumes instead), so those declared event-contract fields can drift while every test passes; PrivateEvent.ExactlyOne and the missing topology/authority policies are likewise unbound. The claimed immutable/versioned dialect is also an exported mutable struct with a mutable slice, while DialectComponent hashes only name/version: changing layout, event, topology, or limits leaves the composed digest unchanged unless callers manually remember a version bump. Model the full note-level policy as an immutable or canonically frozen value, make its semantic configuration participate in or be mechanically bound to the dialect identity, and add differential consistency cases that exercise every load-bearing event, layout, topology, authority, and limit field against profile behavior.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:f5bd3feb8f660196a545a34b26812dad3c6de046","head":"5bea80cbe72f41a